# --- Discovery (local only, never deployed) ---
# Used by `npm run discover:headed` on your own machine. Copy to .env.
ELIXIA_EMAIL=you@example.com
ELIXIA_PASSWORD=your-password
ELIXIA_BASE_URL=https://www.elixia.fi

# --- App ---
# You do not write these into a file by hand. Set them in Vercel (Production,
# Preview and Development), then run `npx vercel env pull .env.local` to get
# them locally.
#
# Supplied automatically once you add Postgres from Vercel's Storage tab and
# enable Neon Auth — do not set these yourself:
#
# DATABASE_URL=                          # Neon pooled connection string
# NEON_AUTH_BASE_URL=                    # your Neon Auth instance
#
# Yours to add in Vercel. Nothing provisions these, and the app refuses to
# serve a signed-in request without ENCRYPTION_KEY:
#
# NEON_AUTH_COOKIE_SECRET=               # openssl rand -base64 32 (min 32 chars)
# ENCRYPTION_KEY=                        # openssl rand -base64 32
#
# QStash is the only thing that books classes: it publishes a delayed HTTP call
# per release instant and fires /api/cron/tick. All four are required together —
# missing one means messages get published and every delivery 401s. The signing
# keys and the token come from the Upstash console; APP_URL is this deployment's
# own public origin. The nightly reindex schedule itself is created once by
# curl, outside the repo — see README "Scheduling that lives outside the repo".
#
# QSTASH_TOKEN=                          # Upstash console → QStash
# QSTASH_CURRENT_SIGNING_KEY=            # Upstash console → QStash → Signing keys
# QSTASH_NEXT_SIGNING_KEY=               # same place; both, for key rotation
# APP_URL=                               # e.g. https://booker.example
#
# Notifications. Email is the default channel and needs the first two; Telegram
# needs the other three, and its webhook must also be registered once with
# setWebhook.
#
# RESEND_API_KEY=                        # optional; no key, no email alerts
# NOTIFY_FROM_EMAIL=                     # e.g. "Elixia Booker <bot@yourdomain>"
# TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=                    # optional
# TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME=                 # bot's @username, without the @
# TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET=               # openssl rand -hex 32
# MOCK_ELIXIA=1                          # 0 once lib/elixia.ts is filled in
# DRY_RUN=1                              # 0 to actually book
#
# There is no DEFAULT_BOOKING_WINDOW_DAYS and no DEFAULT_TIMEZONE. Both were
# per-deployment defaults, so every account started out claiming the same
# membership tier and the same city — a claim nobody made and nothing checks.
# Each account chooses instead, on the setup pages it cannot get past.
